  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nRegional radar shows an area of showers and thunderstorms across the \neastern Dakotas into north central Minnesota early this morning. For \nthe most part, this precip has only brushed our northernmost \ncounties. As the low levels continue to saturate and a 40-50kt LLJ \npersists, rain will fill in across western Minnesota over the \nnext couple of hours. Along and west of a line from Little Falls\nthrough Redwood Falls will see the bulk of the rain through \nlate this morning with anywhere from 0.25 to 0.50\" expected. The\nbetter moisture never really makes it much further east, so \nsouthern and eastern Minnesota will likely see more precip in \nthe form of drizzle than actual rain. Regardless, overcast skies\nwill keep temperatures in the low to mid 60s area wide today. \nMost of the day Friday should be dry before our next chance of \nrain arrives overnight into early Saturday. Another trough tries\nto make its way east, but its progress will be stalled by the \nblocking high over the eastern Great Lakes/Canada that has been \nparked there for the last few days. Additionally, another round \nof dry air will work its way into the region with southeasterly \nflow aloft. Once again, this will inhibit the eastward extent of\nany showers. \n\nThe second half of the weekend, we finally start to see a cutoff low \nover northern Saskatchewan and Manitoba and a system along the east \ncoast break down the stubborn surface high. A longwave trough comes \nashore in the Pacific Northwest to start the work week and looks to \nbring yet another round of precip chances our way late Monday into \nTuesday. Of these three systems (this morning, Saturday, and \nMonday/Tuesday), this last one looks to be the strongest. This will \nlikely lead to a more widespread rain event with a broad area of \n0.50\" to 1\" QPF totals, but the risk of excessive rainfall and \nsevere weather both remain low. Model spread is still fairly high at \nthis point in time, but this will be something to keep an eye on \nduring the final days of September.",
